00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:09.300
ROHIIT TALREJA: My
name is Rohiit Talreja.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.850
I'm a product manager on
the Google Cloud Health Care

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:13.500
and Life Sciences team.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:15.480
I focus specifically
on data governance,

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.790
so I hope it makes sense
why I'm giving the Security

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:19.960
and Compliance
session here today.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:20.820
Thanks for joining.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.660
I know it's a little bit
late in the afternoon.

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:23.702
Thanks for staying awake.

00:00:23.702 --> 00:00:26.260
I hope to keep you that way.

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:31.110
So today, I'm here to talk
about what we call the shared

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:32.409
responsibility model.

00:00:32.409 --> 00:00:35.460
And what that means is
when we have customers

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:37.170
who are using a
Cloud service, it's

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:40.030
a little bit different than
older models of infrastructure,

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:41.050
such as on-premise.

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:43.170
So when we think about
the shared security model,

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:45.030
there exist some
responsibilities

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:47.760
that fall to the Cloud provider
and some responsibilities that

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:49.650
fall to the user,
or the customer.

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:52.500
And this is the frame of
reference for the talk today.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:55.500
We'll be going over how Google
thinks about health care

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:58.860
compliance with a focus
probably on HIPAA,

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:01.440
and then talking about
what you as our users

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:04.260
can do with that context,
and what else you

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.120
may have to think
about as you build,

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.310
design, and secure your
workloads on Google

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:11.580
Cloud for health care data.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:15.250
So just setting a
little bit of context,

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:19.510
health care data is under
duress as I call it.

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:22.170
Health care organizations
are experiencing, on average,

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:24.570
more than twice the
number of attacks

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:27.690
compared to organizations in
other vertical categories.

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:31.230
When we talk about attacks,
what are we talking about?

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:34.890
But before we get to that, let's
talk about why this matters.

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:37.920
The cost of a data
breach is going up

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:39.670
year over year over year.

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:43.860
So a study that was conducted
between February of 2017

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:47.610
and April of 2018 calculated
that the average cost of a data

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:49.980
breach was just
under $4 million.

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.150
So what does this cover?

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:53.160
This covers the
loss of customers

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:54.900
due to reputation loss.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:59.280
This covers the data that was
affected, potentially fines

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.380
coming over that, the
cost of forensics,

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:04.140
the cost of communication
to regulators, to customers,

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:09.780
to affected parties, and of
course, long-term damages.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:11.640
And long-term
damages to reputation

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:15.370
being the primary
motivator of this cost.

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:17.610
So when we talk about
causes of data breach,

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:21.330
it's also good to know
who is experiencing them.

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:25.440
So this startling number,
over 90% of health care

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:29.280
organizations have
experienced a breach

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:31.560
within the last three years.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:34.380
And 50% of health
care organizations

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:36.750
have suffered five
or more breaches

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:38.430
in that same time frame.

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:41.550
So when I say health care
data is under duress,

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:43.320
I hope this now comes across.

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:45.570
And again, it's
important to point out

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:48.400
that when we say a breach,
it's not necessarily hacking.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.080
It's not necessarily malicious.

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:52.590
It could be a doctor
sending a fax--

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:54.840
yes, a fax-- to
the wrong office.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:58.110
It could be a patient sending
data from their doctor

00:02:58.110 --> 00:02:59.110
to a third party.

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:02.370
It could be a doctor
not disposing of records

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:04.900
appropriately when that
patient leaves the system.

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:08.260
So it's important to say that
a breach is a loaded term.

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:09.990
It's both malicious
and anything that's

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:13.440
defined in the regulation.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:16.652
So let's talk more about
costs, specifically distributed

00:03:16.652 --> 00:03:19.110
denial of service attacks,
which are becoming more and more

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:20.200
popular over time.

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.827
We all remember the distributed
denial of service attacks

00:03:22.827 --> 00:03:24.660
that took place in the
last couple of years.

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:27.090
They crippled a number
of institutions.

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:31.350
They cost about $2
million a piece.

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:34.410
And every 40 seconds, a
health care organization

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:37.320
is hit with a ransomware attack.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:41.700
And the same assessment
said that 1 in 6 health care

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:43.290
organizations are affected.

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:49.340
Now that I've scared
you a little bit,

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:51.380
let's scare you a
little bit more.

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:53.750
So when we think about the
number of attacks going up,

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:56.060
one would assume that
the number of protections

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:58.850
is also rising to meet
that increase in attacks.

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:00.950
Actually, it's
unfortunately the opposite.

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:03.620
Cybersecurity budgets in
health care organizations

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:06.967
have dropped to just
about 3% of total spend.

00:04:06.967 --> 00:04:09.050
This is not to say that
health care organizations'

00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:10.850
budgets are decreasing overall.

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:13.970
Actually, the amount that health
care organizations are spending

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:16.110
is increasing year over year.

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:19.220
However, the amount spent on
cybersecurity is constant,

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:21.890
meaning that in
proportion, less money

00:04:21.890 --> 00:04:27.860
is being spent on
increasingly important areas.

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:30.020
So why is health
care a prime target?

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:31.620
I think most people
would know this,

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:33.620
but we'll talk a little
bit into specifics.

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:35.430
Health care data is rich.

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:37.100
And when I say the
data is rich, it

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:40.850
means that I can get data
about multiple aspects

00:04:40.850 --> 00:04:44.270
of an individual just
by breaching one system.

00:04:44.270 --> 00:04:46.340
So if we think about
your email account,

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:48.620
maybe that has some personal
identifying information.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:50.828
Your bank account maybe has
some personal identifying

00:04:50.828 --> 00:04:51.372
information.

00:04:51.372 --> 00:04:53.330
But really, when you
think about those security

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:54.620
questionnaires you fill in--

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:55.700
what was your
mother's maiden name?

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:57.012
What street did you grow up in?

00:04:57.012 --> 00:04:58.220
Where were your parents born?

00:04:58.220 --> 00:04:59.632
Where were you born?

00:04:59.632 --> 00:05:02.090
There's really only one place
where all of that information

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:02.800
is--

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:04.280
health care records.

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:06.560
And that means that
the total, let's say,

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:09.350
cost-benefit analysis
to a hacker looking for,

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:11.810
where do I spend
my hacking moneys

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:16.100
and time, health care data is
less protected and more useful.

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:18.140
Therefore, let's look at that.

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:20.690
The other reason why health
care data is a prime target

00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:23.690
is because their data is stored
primarily on legacy systems.

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:25.690
So we know cybersecurity
budgets are going down.

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:26.690
We talked about that.

00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:30.050
Systems are older, easier
to breach, theoretically.

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:32.510
And lastly, there is
a specific category

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:35.420
of hackers who
hack for fun, hack

00:05:35.420 --> 00:05:37.160
to cause the most
damage possible.

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:39.590
And when we look at health
care organizations taken down

00:05:39.590 --> 00:05:41.300
by things like
WannaCry, taken down

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:43.490
by things like database
attacks, downtime

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:45.350
impacts patient safety.

00:05:45.350 --> 00:05:47.450
And therefore, people
who get pleasure

00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:49.100
out of taking down
these organizations

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:51.530
are looking to health care
because unfortunately, it

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:53.040
causes a lot of damage.

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:55.730
So where can we go from here?

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:57.980
And as I said, this
talk will be focused

00:05:57.980 --> 00:06:01.730
on both what Google is doing
in the health care compliance

00:06:01.730 --> 00:06:05.210
and security space and what
you, as users of Google Cloud,

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:10.280
can do to help prevent all
of these types of attacks.

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:13.910
So level setting, I know
this audience probably

00:06:13.910 --> 00:06:16.580
knows more than the average
audience about what HIPAA is

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:17.550
and what it entails.

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:21.530
But HIPAA, US regulation for the
protection of health care data

00:06:21.530 --> 00:06:23.330
known as Protected
Health Information.

00:06:23.330 --> 00:06:25.160
So if you hear me
use the acronym PHI

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:26.840
throughout this
talk, I'm referring

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:30.170
to Health Care Information
as defined under HIPAA.

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:32.690
But we can probably
generalize that to mean

00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:35.840
any sensitive health care
information globally.

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:38.810
The same sort of
protections would apply.

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:42.403
So HIPAA is broken into three
sections of requirements.

00:06:42.403 --> 00:06:43.820
We have administrative
safeguards,

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:45.470
which is basically how
you run your business,

00:06:45.470 --> 00:06:47.053
how you hire people,
how you give them

00:06:47.053 --> 00:06:49.440
access to systems, what
they do with those systems,

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:51.410
whether you plan
for disaster events.

00:06:51.410 --> 00:06:53.510
And then we have
physical safeguards.

00:06:53.510 --> 00:06:55.850
It's like, when do you let
people into your buildings?

00:06:55.850 --> 00:06:57.410
Where do you store
your IT systems?

00:06:57.410 --> 00:06:59.270
Who gets access to
your IT systems?

00:06:59.270 --> 00:07:02.720
Before you give a vendor
access to your server closet,

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:05.425
what sort of processes
are in place to make sure

00:07:05.425 --> 00:07:07.550
that they're properly vetted
and they're only doing

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:09.080
what they should be doing?

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:10.738
And lastly, the
biggest bucket, which

00:07:10.738 --> 00:07:12.530
is where I'll spend
most of the time today,

00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:13.940
is technical safeguards.

00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:17.870
So HIPAA, being a
slightly older law,

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:21.060
was not necessarily created
for the cloud environment.

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:24.140
However, it has been
amended a couple of times

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:26.390
to account for higher
tech activities.

00:07:26.390 --> 00:07:28.880
And it has been interpreted
by the government

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:31.940
and by industry
groups to make sure

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:33.890
that the technical
safeguards under HIPAA

00:07:33.890 --> 00:07:38.790
have somewhat caught up with
growing technology trends.

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:41.340
So in terms of what we'll
be talking about today,

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:44.030
we'll talk about identity and
access control, encryption

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:46.520
and transmission protection,
audit logging, activity

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:50.310
logging, and audit controls.

00:07:50.310 --> 00:07:52.820
So I'll touch upon these both
first from the Google side

00:07:52.820 --> 00:07:55.160
and then from the customer side.

00:07:55.160 --> 00:08:00.050
So it's important to
talk about the HIPAA BAA

00:08:00.050 --> 00:08:01.100
when talking about HIPAA.

00:08:01.100 --> 00:08:03.290
The Business Associate
Agreement, or BAA,

00:08:03.290 --> 00:08:05.540
is the contract that
formalizes the requirements

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:08.750
between the service provider,
the business associate,

00:08:08.750 --> 00:08:09.770
and the covered entity--

00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:11.930
generally, the insurance
plan, the provider system,

00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:13.940
or the health care
information clearing house.

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:16.250
And this BAA basically
formalizes the relationship

00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:20.840
and says both parties agree that
HIPAA data is being exchanged

00:08:20.840 --> 00:08:21.980
in this contract.

00:08:21.980 --> 00:08:23.600
And here are the
security requirements

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:25.683
that are in place that
govern the use, protection,

00:08:25.683 --> 00:08:27.560
transmission of that
health care data.

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:30.410
It's important to note
that Google Cloud is

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:33.760
one of the few
providers that offers

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:37.039
a rigorous Enterprise-grade
BAA that covers

00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:39.380
a large number of GCP services.

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:41.210
And even more than
that, we do it

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:42.860
at no additional
cost to the customer

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:48.190
because we believe that security
in health care is not optional.

00:08:48.190 --> 00:08:50.710
We shouldn't give
you fewer protections

00:08:50.710 --> 00:08:53.410
just because you're in health
care and have a tight budget.

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:56.140
So all of our protections in
the BAA are there by default.

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:57.070
There is no upcharge.

00:08:57.070 --> 00:09:00.160
And specifically, it includes
any region, any instance size

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:02.590
that's covered for the
service in the BAA,

00:09:02.590 --> 00:09:06.010
and has all the protections
around breach notification

00:09:06.010 --> 00:09:09.670
and encryption by default.

00:09:09.670 --> 00:09:12.100
So going into detail,
administrative safeguards.

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:13.900
What are they?

00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:16.510
This is Google's
security program.

00:09:16.510 --> 00:09:20.470
We design our security
system with an approach

00:09:20.470 --> 00:09:22.630
we call defense
in depth, whereas

00:09:22.630 --> 00:09:25.870
the traditional security model
is have a hard perimeter.

00:09:25.870 --> 00:09:29.500
Make it very hard for people
to get on to your network

00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:33.400
using things like tough
firewalls, VPNs, bastion hosts,

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:36.370
dedicated machines that
can access that network.

00:09:36.370 --> 00:09:38.350
But think of it
like an eggshell.

00:09:38.350 --> 00:09:41.410
If you have a hard perimeter,
but then once you're inside,

00:09:41.410 --> 00:09:42.610
a gooey center.

00:09:42.610 --> 00:09:44.590
So anybody who gets
onto that network

00:09:44.590 --> 00:09:46.600
can then have free
access to anything

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:50.560
that is on the network, your EHR
systems, your billing systems.

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:51.130
You name it.

00:09:51.130 --> 00:09:52.630
Anything that's on
the network, they

00:09:52.630 --> 00:09:54.400
have if they can
compromise your network.

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:57.460
Instead, Google enforces
defense in depth

00:09:57.460 --> 00:09:59.620
by putting different
sorts of protections

00:09:59.620 --> 00:10:02.440
that are relevant to different
attacks at different levels

00:10:02.440 --> 00:10:03.890
of the infrastructure.

00:10:03.890 --> 00:10:05.860
So for example, when
we talk about hardware,

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:09.940
Google designs and plans our
own server hardware, storage

00:10:09.940 --> 00:10:11.260
hardware, compute hardware.

00:10:11.260 --> 00:10:14.470
And by doing so, not only do we
control the exact performance

00:10:14.470 --> 00:10:17.230
of the hardware to meet the
specifications that we need,

00:10:17.230 --> 00:10:19.960
we can also cut out
unnecessary components

00:10:19.960 --> 00:10:22.240
and control our
supply chain down

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:24.940
to off-the-shelf
components, reducing "vendor

00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:27.040
in the middle" risk.

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:29.800
Taking it all the
way to the top layer,

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:33.370
we have a robust
identity layer in place

00:10:33.370 --> 00:10:36.640
that only allows approved
individuals to access services,

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:38.410
and only services that
have been approved

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:41.800
to communicate with each
other to perform activities

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:43.010
with each other.

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:45.280
So I won't talk through all
of this slide in detail,

00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:47.697
but this is just trying to say
there is a defense in depth

00:10:47.697 --> 00:10:51.880
approach that is fundamental to
everything we do in security,

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:54.700
down from our culture to
our technical controls

00:10:54.700 --> 00:10:55.660
and our operations.

00:10:58.940 --> 00:11:01.660
I think this slide,
hopefully, should give you

00:11:01.660 --> 00:11:04.450
an idea that we understand
security certifications.

00:11:04.450 --> 00:11:05.650
Table stakes.

00:11:05.650 --> 00:11:07.420
As part of the
administrative actions

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:09.610
of running a secure
compliance program,

00:11:09.610 --> 00:11:12.010
Google has gone out and
gotten certifications

00:11:12.010 --> 00:11:14.080
that are relevant to
us as a cloud provider,

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:17.800
such as ISO, which is
fundamental cloud security

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:21.542
and privacy certifications,
as well as certifications

00:11:21.542 --> 00:11:23.500
that are specific to our
customers, which helps

00:11:23.500 --> 00:11:24.940
them grow on Google Cloud.

00:11:24.940 --> 00:11:26.260
So this is things like HITRUST.

00:11:26.260 --> 00:11:28.060
This is things like FedRAMP.

00:11:28.060 --> 00:11:31.660
Basically, certifications
where any vendor in the chain

00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:33.820
needs to be certified
so that the one that's

00:11:33.820 --> 00:11:36.550
providing the final service
to the end customer can get

00:11:36.550 --> 00:11:40.000
certified as well.

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:42.130
Continuing onto
administrative activities,

00:11:42.130 --> 00:11:45.400
Google regularly conducts
disaster recovery drills

00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:48.220
by simulating real
world scenarios.

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:51.190
So these could be a fake
earthquake that potentially

00:11:51.190 --> 00:11:53.920
knocks out power to a data
center, which of course we

00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:57.190
simulate, hopefully.

00:11:57.190 --> 00:11:59.890
Dropping the power to the
internet, things like that.

00:11:59.890 --> 00:12:02.480
There was a day in the office
where I showed up to work.

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:04.690
The video conferencing
cut mid-meeting.

00:12:04.690 --> 00:12:07.510
Somebody sent a text message
that said, oh, by the way,

00:12:07.510 --> 00:12:09.580
the internet is down for
the next three hours.

00:12:09.580 --> 00:12:10.417
Enjoy.

00:12:10.417 --> 00:12:12.250
Tell us what you did
so we can write it down

00:12:12.250 --> 00:12:13.960
for the actual
plan going forward.

00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:16.060
And I say this as
a cavalier story,

00:12:16.060 --> 00:12:19.150
but really, these
tests are designed

00:12:19.150 --> 00:12:21.760
to give teams the
ability to respond

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:23.230
to real-world scenarios.

00:12:23.230 --> 00:12:26.590
And how they respond to the
test tests their plan of action

00:12:26.590 --> 00:12:28.990
and also helps them improve
their plan of action

00:12:28.990 --> 00:12:30.520
for when real incidents occur.

00:12:34.220 --> 00:12:37.280
So talking about, again,
administrative actions.

00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:40.310
What is Google doing to
run a proper HIPAA program,

00:12:40.310 --> 00:12:42.330
proper health care
compliance program?

00:12:42.330 --> 00:12:44.960
This is the list of services
covered by the BAA as

00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:47.990
of some time last week.

00:12:47.990 --> 00:12:50.120
I would probably
be willing to say

00:12:50.120 --> 00:12:52.940
that it's already out of date,
in that we continue to add more

00:12:52.940 --> 00:12:55.280
services to the BAA over time.

00:12:55.280 --> 00:12:56.655
They're being
added very rapidly.

00:12:56.655 --> 00:12:58.322
There's probably a
couple more that have

00:12:58.322 --> 00:12:59.600
been added in the last week.

00:13:04.150 --> 00:13:08.230
So when we talk about physical
safeguards, facilities

00:13:08.230 --> 00:13:08.870
management.

00:13:08.870 --> 00:13:11.710
So physical safeguards, I
won't spend too much time

00:13:11.710 --> 00:13:15.078
talking about because it
really just depends on,

00:13:15.078 --> 00:13:16.370
how do we run our data centers?

00:13:16.370 --> 00:13:17.530
How do we run our offices?

00:13:17.530 --> 00:13:19.030
When we talk about
our data centers,

00:13:19.030 --> 00:13:22.030
our data centers are some of
the most protected buildings

00:13:22.030 --> 00:13:23.230
in the world.

00:13:23.230 --> 00:13:25.540
They're protected by
custom-designed electronic

00:13:25.540 --> 00:13:29.350
access cards, alarms, perimeter
defenses, laser-beam detection.

00:13:29.350 --> 00:13:31.330
Imagine a heist movie.

00:13:31.330 --> 00:13:34.093
Anything you see in that heist
movie, we probably have it.

00:13:34.093 --> 00:13:36.010
And there's probably
some other stuff in there

00:13:36.010 --> 00:13:37.780
that they won't
show in the movies.

00:13:37.780 --> 00:13:41.170
It's safe to say facility
access is tightly controlled.

00:13:41.170 --> 00:13:43.870
Only approved employees
and their guests,

00:13:43.870 --> 00:13:46.330
and vendors,
potentially-- people

00:13:46.330 --> 00:13:47.800
who should be there are there.

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:49.342
They're vetted before
they get there.

00:13:49.342 --> 00:13:51.970
Background checks
are done, and so on.

00:13:51.970 --> 00:13:54.290
Technical safeguards.

00:13:54.290 --> 00:13:55.330
Defense in depth.

00:13:55.330 --> 00:13:56.410
I talked about this.

00:13:56.410 --> 00:13:59.200
This just shows our
fundamental approach

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:03.260
to technical safeguards, and
then going into some detail.

00:14:03.260 --> 00:14:05.290
We have encryption
by default at rest.

00:14:05.290 --> 00:14:08.150
And when I say by
default, what do I mean?

00:14:08.150 --> 00:14:09.610
What I mean is
customers don't have

00:14:09.610 --> 00:14:12.980
to check a box that says
turn on default encryption.

00:14:12.980 --> 00:14:15.460
They don't have to
say what size keys

00:14:15.460 --> 00:14:17.643
they want, what
level of encryption,

00:14:17.643 --> 00:14:19.810
what data stores are in
scope for encryption or not.

00:14:19.810 --> 00:14:20.350
No.

00:14:20.350 --> 00:14:23.620
Data on rest on Google
Cloud Platform is encrypted.

00:14:23.620 --> 00:14:26.870
This is a sample of one
layer of encryption.

00:14:26.870 --> 00:14:29.740
So different services,
different storage applications

00:14:29.740 --> 00:14:32.090
have maybe multiple
levels of encryption.

00:14:32.090 --> 00:14:35.470
This just shows an individual
file coming into Google Cloud.

00:14:35.470 --> 00:14:37.150
And what happens
here is that file

00:14:37.150 --> 00:14:38.990
is broken into multiple pieces.

00:14:38.990 --> 00:14:42.190
Each of these pieces is
encrypted with its own key.

00:14:42.190 --> 00:14:45.610
Then we wrap that key
with a key encryption key

00:14:45.610 --> 00:14:48.010
so that we don't store
keys in plain text.

00:14:48.010 --> 00:14:49.600
The keys are also encrypted.

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:52.810
And then each of these files
and their encryption key

00:14:52.810 --> 00:14:56.530
is put into a different
physical system in many cases.

00:14:56.530 --> 00:14:59.290
So this is basically saying
data is not only encrypted,

00:14:59.290 --> 00:15:01.840
it's split up, encrypted,
and then distributed

00:15:01.840 --> 00:15:04.510
across the infrastructure
so that a failure in any one

00:15:04.510 --> 00:15:07.090
machine or multiple
machines does not compromise

00:15:07.090 --> 00:15:08.440
the integrity of the file.

00:15:08.440 --> 00:15:12.850
It also means that if, for
some hypothetical case,

00:15:12.850 --> 00:15:16.450
somebody got physical access
to one machine, the probability

00:15:16.450 --> 00:15:20.673
that, A, there is an entire file
on that machine is low, and B,

00:15:20.673 --> 00:15:22.840
all of the chunks are
encrypted with different keys.

00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:25.630
So the attack surface,
again, defense in depth

00:15:25.630 --> 00:15:28.630
is lowered with every step.

00:15:28.630 --> 00:15:32.800
So when we talk about endpoint
security, Google, on our end,

00:15:32.800 --> 00:15:35.890
we use devices that
are updated centrally.

00:15:35.890 --> 00:15:37.210
They auto update.

00:15:37.210 --> 00:15:39.610
They make sure that
there are strong security

00:15:39.610 --> 00:15:41.120
primitives on the device.

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:43.265
And we also make these
devices and softwares

00:15:43.265 --> 00:15:44.890
available to our
customers, which we'll

00:15:44.890 --> 00:15:47.170
talk a little bit about later.

00:15:47.170 --> 00:15:51.820
And when we talk about defense
in depth, one of the easiest

00:15:51.820 --> 00:15:55.090
things an organization can do
to secure their technology,

00:15:55.090 --> 00:15:58.060
to secure their systems, is
have two-factor or multi-factor

00:15:58.060 --> 00:15:59.480
authentication in place.

00:15:59.480 --> 00:16:01.840
This helps prevent
against phishing.

00:16:01.840 --> 00:16:05.200
This helps add an additional
layer of auditing and logging,

00:16:05.200 --> 00:16:07.030
and basically make
it so that the attack

00:16:07.030 --> 00:16:08.350
surface is, again, reduced.

00:16:10.960 --> 00:16:14.050
So now that we've talked a
little bit of an overview

00:16:14.050 --> 00:16:17.110
into how Google secures
our production systems

00:16:17.110 --> 00:16:20.050
and makes services
available to customers,

00:16:20.050 --> 00:16:22.600
I want to talk a little
bit about how customers

00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:24.670
should think about
their controls that

00:16:24.670 --> 00:16:26.360
are available to them.

00:16:26.360 --> 00:16:28.990
Not everything is going to be
handled by the Cloud provider,

00:16:28.990 --> 00:16:31.720
because Google wants to give
customers the flexibility

00:16:31.720 --> 00:16:34.130
to build the systems
that it needs to build.

00:16:34.130 --> 00:16:36.515
We don't know what data you
want, data schema you want.

00:16:36.515 --> 00:16:38.140
We don't know where
you want it stored.

00:16:38.140 --> 00:16:40.100
We don't know what
network setup you want.

00:16:40.100 --> 00:16:42.700
But what we do want
to do is give you

00:16:42.700 --> 00:16:45.700
the controls to do what
it is you need to do.

00:16:48.370 --> 00:16:51.640
So I have a similar diagram.

00:16:51.640 --> 00:16:54.910
Our philosophy of
defense in depth

00:16:54.910 --> 00:16:57.760
transcends our own
sphere of influence.

00:16:57.760 --> 00:17:01.450
And we would like to give
customers that same ability.

00:17:01.450 --> 00:17:05.290
So customers are working on
potentially the same level

00:17:05.290 --> 00:17:06.790
of controls that we are.

00:17:06.790 --> 00:17:09.310
Customers care about
infrastructure security

00:17:09.310 --> 00:17:13.702
in some cases, but in our case,
we've taken care of that one.

00:17:13.702 --> 00:17:15.410
So customers care
about network security.

00:17:15.410 --> 00:17:17.577
They care about data security,
application security,

00:17:17.577 --> 00:17:20.690
identity and access management
endpoints, monitoring

00:17:20.690 --> 00:17:21.650
and operations.

00:17:21.650 --> 00:17:24.220
And then, of course, wrapping
it nice and tight together

00:17:24.220 --> 00:17:26.440
in that governance, risk,
and compliance framework.

00:17:26.440 --> 00:17:29.715
So the way I think about this
left column of governance,

00:17:29.715 --> 00:17:31.090
risk, and compliance
is basically

00:17:31.090 --> 00:17:33.940
good documentation and
policies around everything else

00:17:33.940 --> 00:17:35.270
on the slide.

00:17:35.270 --> 00:17:38.650
So if you don't have good
data security, for example,

00:17:38.650 --> 00:17:42.460
your compliance is going
to be hard for you.

00:17:42.460 --> 00:17:45.130
So this is the
framework of protections

00:17:45.130 --> 00:17:49.180
that are in place, diving
into a little bit more detail.

00:17:49.180 --> 00:17:51.940
But first, touching
again on shared security,

00:17:51.940 --> 00:17:54.730
because I really think that's
a fundamental consideration

00:17:54.730 --> 00:17:55.930
for this.

00:17:55.930 --> 00:17:58.600
When we think about our
infrastructure services,

00:17:58.600 --> 00:18:00.760
Google has taken care
of the hardware, how

00:18:00.760 --> 00:18:04.340
the underlying hardware
boots, what kernel it has,

00:18:04.340 --> 00:18:06.620
how the data is
encrypted and stored,

00:18:06.620 --> 00:18:08.070
how the audit logging is done.

00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:10.400
But then when we
make that guest OS,

00:18:10.400 --> 00:18:12.950
that is the layer at
which the controls become

00:18:12.950 --> 00:18:14.060
available to the customer.

00:18:14.060 --> 00:18:14.930
Do they want Linux?

00:18:14.930 --> 00:18:16.160
Do they want Windows?

00:18:16.160 --> 00:18:18.830
Do they want something
else, something proprietary?

00:18:18.830 --> 00:18:20.630
And that's the level
of control to which

00:18:20.630 --> 00:18:25.370
customers start at on our
infrastructure services.

00:18:25.370 --> 00:18:27.320
If you don't want
that level of control,

00:18:27.320 --> 00:18:30.680
if you don't need that level of
flexibility for every system,

00:18:30.680 --> 00:18:32.630
why not take advantage
of one of the platform

00:18:32.630 --> 00:18:33.920
as a service solutions?

00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:37.130
And that's because Google has,
again, made more sane choices

00:18:37.130 --> 00:18:39.560
about the security of those
systems all the way up

00:18:39.560 --> 00:18:42.260
to potentially the
application itself.

00:18:42.260 --> 00:18:44.960
In which case, you can drop
in your business logic,

00:18:44.960 --> 00:18:48.640
and rest assured that
the underlying features

00:18:48.640 --> 00:18:49.640
have been accounted for.

00:18:49.640 --> 00:18:52.190
And you have less and less
responsibility over time.

00:18:52.190 --> 00:18:54.950
And this translates also into
our software as a service

00:18:54.950 --> 00:18:58.165
solution, so G Suite, which
encompasses Gmail, Drive.

00:18:58.165 --> 00:19:00.290
Basically, the only thing
you're doing in that case

00:19:00.290 --> 00:19:04.010
is managing the data, managing
who can access the data,

00:19:04.010 --> 00:19:06.410
and managing who you
share the data with.

00:19:08.960 --> 00:19:11.360
So going into those
same categories,

00:19:11.360 --> 00:19:13.430
where do customer
responsibilities

00:19:13.430 --> 00:19:17.820
lie in identity and access?

00:19:17.820 --> 00:19:21.632
First, we should talk about
what that means on Google Cloud.

00:19:21.632 --> 00:19:23.340
I think we can get a
little bit into some

00:19:23.340 --> 00:19:24.940
of the technical details here.

00:19:24.940 --> 00:19:29.280
We can say that on
Google Cloud, there are

00:19:29.280 --> 00:19:30.612
two main types of identities.

00:19:30.612 --> 00:19:32.070
We can have a human
identity, which

00:19:32.070 --> 00:19:34.220
is me doing work as myself.

00:19:34.220 --> 00:19:36.180
And we can also have
service accounts, or what

00:19:36.180 --> 00:19:37.860
we call robot identities.

00:19:37.860 --> 00:19:42.930
That's like some service doing
work on behalf of a system,

00:19:42.930 --> 00:19:47.580
on behalf of a group of people,
serving an API, doing Cron jobs

00:19:47.580 --> 00:19:48.510
on data.

00:19:48.510 --> 00:19:51.330
Basically, things that a
human wanted to automate,

00:19:51.330 --> 00:19:53.820
so they let a machine do it
periodically, or a machine

00:19:53.820 --> 00:19:56.430
do it for the scope of security.

00:19:56.430 --> 00:19:59.420
The way these accounts
authenticate is different.

00:19:59.420 --> 00:20:02.310
So when you have a human
account, you log onto GCP.

00:20:02.310 --> 00:20:04.280
You'll type in your
username and password.

00:20:04.280 --> 00:20:07.030
You'll enter, hopefully, as
you should, your second factor

00:20:07.030 --> 00:20:08.910
or third factor
of authentication.

00:20:08.910 --> 00:20:11.340
And then you will
function as yourself.

00:20:11.340 --> 00:20:13.410
When you want to
authenticate to--

00:20:13.410 --> 00:20:15.510
when a service account
wants to authenticate,

00:20:15.510 --> 00:20:16.680
it's provided a key.

00:20:16.680 --> 00:20:20.580
And that key basically
says, this service account

00:20:20.580 --> 00:20:24.150
is accredited for this
organization for the scope of--

00:20:24.150 --> 00:20:26.900
performing this
scope of operations.

00:20:26.900 --> 00:20:29.730
And it's important to
note that at some point,

00:20:29.730 --> 00:20:31.080
things become similar.

00:20:31.080 --> 00:20:33.600
Both humans and service
accounts need IAM roles,

00:20:33.600 --> 00:20:36.870
IAM permissions on resources
in order to modify them,

00:20:36.870 --> 00:20:40.030
in order to use them,
things like that.

00:20:40.030 --> 00:20:42.457
So a key concept here--

00:20:42.457 --> 00:20:44.790
different types of identity,
different types of accounts

00:20:44.790 --> 00:20:46.590
are relevant to
different applications.

00:20:46.590 --> 00:20:48.600
If you're doing an
admin operation,

00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:50.820
you might want to use a
human account because it's

00:20:50.820 --> 00:20:52.980
an ad hoc, one-off thing.

00:20:52.980 --> 00:20:55.770
If you're automating something,
if you're providing a service,

00:20:55.770 --> 00:21:00.270
scoping down the exact service
operations to a robot account

00:21:00.270 --> 00:21:01.710
helps maintain security.

00:21:01.710 --> 00:21:04.620
And it helps maintain security
because that robot is scoped

00:21:04.620 --> 00:21:06.720
to only a limited
set of duties and it

00:21:06.720 --> 00:21:08.070
can't override that scope.

00:21:11.840 --> 00:21:13.400
Taking this one step further.

00:21:13.400 --> 00:21:16.160
Now, we've talked about
manipulating resources

00:21:16.160 --> 00:21:17.390
on Google Cloud.

00:21:17.390 --> 00:21:19.850
What about securing
applications?

00:21:19.850 --> 00:21:22.775
So Google takes the identity
model one step further

00:21:22.775 --> 00:21:24.650
and has made something
available to customers

00:21:24.650 --> 00:21:26.570
called Identity-Aware Proxy.

00:21:26.570 --> 00:21:30.020
And what this is is a Load
Balancer and proxy that

00:21:30.020 --> 00:21:33.780
sits in front of applications.

00:21:33.780 --> 00:21:36.950
And when a user
request comes in,

00:21:36.950 --> 00:21:40.550
the IAP basically does a
check for who that user is,

00:21:40.550 --> 00:21:43.070
what they're entitled to
do on the application,

00:21:43.070 --> 00:21:45.270
and those things have to match.

00:21:45.270 --> 00:21:48.590
So not only does it do
identity-based controls,

00:21:48.590 --> 00:21:50.400
it also does context controls.

00:21:50.400 --> 00:21:52.280
Is this user
accessing the service

00:21:52.280 --> 00:21:54.170
from an approved IP address?

00:21:54.170 --> 00:21:57.800
Are they accessing the service
from an approved partner

00:21:57.800 --> 00:21:58.590
service?

00:21:58.590 --> 00:22:01.130
So a lot of these checks can
be done at this proxy layer

00:22:01.130 --> 00:22:03.140
and make sure that only
legitimate requests,

00:22:03.140 --> 00:22:07.197
legitimate access can make it
into the back end application.

00:22:10.810 --> 00:22:12.760
So now talking
about user controls

00:22:12.760 --> 00:22:17.260
for encryption, data management,
and transmission protection.

00:22:17.260 --> 00:22:19.270
First, remember
when I showed you

00:22:19.270 --> 00:22:22.990
the diagram about how Google
encrypts data by default?

00:22:22.990 --> 00:22:25.960
Well, it turns out that's only
one of the encryption options

00:22:25.960 --> 00:22:27.280
available on Google Cloud.

00:22:27.280 --> 00:22:29.410
It's the leftmost one here.

00:22:29.410 --> 00:22:31.490
What we call,
default encryption.

00:22:31.490 --> 00:22:35.740
So it's the same diagram we
have on the previous slide, just

00:22:35.740 --> 00:22:38.720
showcasing the other two
options for key management.

00:22:38.720 --> 00:22:42.670
So maybe you want a
little bit more control

00:22:42.670 --> 00:22:44.020
than the default encryption.

00:22:44.020 --> 00:22:46.420
You want to specify
which keys are

00:22:46.420 --> 00:22:50.200
used to encrypt which files, how
often those keys are rotated,

00:22:50.200 --> 00:22:53.770
when those keys are deleted,
and whether any of the keys

00:22:53.770 --> 00:22:55.130
need to be reused over time.

00:22:55.130 --> 00:22:57.400
Basically, the
default encryption

00:22:57.400 --> 00:22:59.110
has made all of those
decisions for you

00:22:59.110 --> 00:23:01.550
and offered it in a
managed service approach.

00:23:01.550 --> 00:23:03.010
But if any one of
those decisions

00:23:03.010 --> 00:23:04.750
sounds like a decision
you need to make,

00:23:04.750 --> 00:23:07.240
that's what customer-managed
encryption keys are for.

00:23:07.240 --> 00:23:09.640
And what you do is you'd
configure the service.

00:23:09.640 --> 00:23:12.100
Say, I would like to
manage the encryption keys.

00:23:12.100 --> 00:23:15.230
When that service needs
to encrypt a file,

00:23:15.230 --> 00:23:19.330
it will go to find
the key you specify,

00:23:19.330 --> 00:23:21.880
encrypt it or decrypt it,
and then put that key back.

00:23:21.880 --> 00:23:24.760
There is an even more
controlled option

00:23:24.760 --> 00:23:26.950
called Customer Supplied Keys.

00:23:26.950 --> 00:23:28.900
And what this enables
you to do is not

00:23:28.900 --> 00:23:32.590
store any of the encryption
keys on Google Cloud.

00:23:32.590 --> 00:23:35.350
So both the first option,
which is default encryption,

00:23:35.350 --> 00:23:38.260
and the second option, customer
managed encryption keys,

00:23:38.260 --> 00:23:41.020
store the encryption keys
in what we call Google Cloud

00:23:41.020 --> 00:23:42.880
KMS, our Key Management System.

00:23:42.880 --> 00:23:45.520
If you want to store the
encryption keys on-prem,

00:23:45.520 --> 00:23:47.410
then you can use
customer-supplied encryption

00:23:47.410 --> 00:23:47.920
keys.

00:23:47.920 --> 00:23:50.320
And the way you would do
that is, in the API request

00:23:50.320 --> 00:23:53.680
to access a file, you
specify the encryption key.

00:23:53.680 --> 00:23:56.407
And that file is
decrypted and sent to you,

00:23:56.407 --> 00:23:57.490
and the key is not stored.

00:23:57.490 --> 00:23:59.440
And the same thing works
when you're storing a file.

00:23:59.440 --> 00:24:00.440
So you give it the file.

00:24:00.440 --> 00:24:02.150
You give it the
encryption key you want.

00:24:02.150 --> 00:24:04.750
We perform our chunking
and encryption the same way

00:24:04.750 --> 00:24:06.743
we normally would,
but we use your key,

00:24:06.743 --> 00:24:08.410
and we destroy it
afterwards so that you

00:24:08.410 --> 00:24:09.640
have to keep supplying it.

00:24:09.640 --> 00:24:11.260
The one thing I will
caveat with this

00:24:11.260 --> 00:24:14.320
is that this can be
difficult to get right.

00:24:14.320 --> 00:24:17.890
It is possible, if you don't
have a robust existing key

00:24:17.890 --> 00:24:21.310
management service on-prem
or on another cloud system--

00:24:21.310 --> 00:24:22.840
wherever you're
storing your keys--

00:24:22.840 --> 00:24:24.820
you can encrypt data.

00:24:24.820 --> 00:24:26.030
We don't store the key.

00:24:26.030 --> 00:24:30.070
And if you don't store that key,
that data is effectively gone.

00:24:30.070 --> 00:24:33.890
So it's important to say that
while this offers more control,

00:24:33.890 --> 00:24:35.893
it also offers more
responsibility.

00:24:38.430 --> 00:24:40.620
So another thing
that you can use

00:24:40.620 --> 00:24:45.060
to control the access and
permissions around data

00:24:45.060 --> 00:24:47.820
on Cloud is VPC
Service Controls.

00:24:47.820 --> 00:24:51.060
And what this allows you to do
is define a security perimeter

00:24:51.060 --> 00:24:54.030
around Google Cloud
Platform resources

00:24:54.030 --> 00:24:56.560
to constrain data
to that perimeter.

00:24:56.560 --> 00:24:59.550
Basically, control when
data leaves or comes

00:24:59.550 --> 00:25:02.040
into that security perimeter
and help mitigate data

00:25:02.040 --> 00:25:04.090
leaving that perimeter.

00:25:04.090 --> 00:25:07.300
So VPC has three main use cases.

00:25:07.300 --> 00:25:09.790
We'll talk about mitigating
data exfiltration.

00:25:09.790 --> 00:25:11.910
So what this diagram
is showing here

00:25:11.910 --> 00:25:13.800
is by setting up the
virtual perimeter,

00:25:13.800 --> 00:25:16.890
you can prevent the number
of exfiltration pathways

00:25:16.890 --> 00:25:19.440
to only what you want.

00:25:19.440 --> 00:25:23.130
So let's say that you
have a GCS bucket,

00:25:23.130 --> 00:25:25.320
and you only want
it to be accessed

00:25:25.320 --> 00:25:28.710
within services in
a specific network,

00:25:28.710 --> 00:25:31.660
but not by any services
outside of that network.

00:25:31.660 --> 00:25:33.420
So what you would do
is you would put--

00:25:33.420 --> 00:25:36.840
configure the VPC--
sorry-- service

00:25:36.840 --> 00:25:40.350
controls so that the services
that should access the data

00:25:40.350 --> 00:25:42.000
are on the same
network, and services

00:25:42.000 --> 00:25:43.770
that shouldn't access the
data are outside that.

00:25:43.770 --> 00:25:45.812
And then configure it such
that it basically auto

00:25:45.812 --> 00:25:51.060
rejects anything coming in that
isn't already on that network.

00:25:51.060 --> 00:25:55.860
This is also a great enabler
for hybrid cloud, hybrid GCP,

00:25:55.860 --> 00:25:59.440
hybrid with other clouds, hybrid
cloud and on-prem, et cetera.

00:25:59.440 --> 00:26:03.773
So you can include on-prem
resources in your VPC network.

00:26:03.773 --> 00:26:05.190
And what that will
allow you to do

00:26:05.190 --> 00:26:07.500
is securely access
resources on Google

00:26:07.500 --> 00:26:10.360
Cloud from your on-premise
environment, and vice versa.

00:26:10.360 --> 00:26:12.690
So this is a really
good security control

00:26:12.690 --> 00:26:15.870
for extending your on-premise
network to cover a Google

00:26:15.870 --> 00:26:18.420
Cloud, again, and vice versa.

00:26:21.150 --> 00:26:25.680
And lastly, combined with
Identity-Aware Proxy,

00:26:25.680 --> 00:26:28.740
VPC is also an important
service for enforcing

00:26:28.740 --> 00:26:30.660
context aware access.

00:26:30.660 --> 00:26:34.830
So in this case, it's not only
the identity of the accessing

00:26:34.830 --> 00:26:36.630
service or the
individual that matters,

00:26:36.630 --> 00:26:40.570
it's also the context for which
they're applying for access.

00:26:40.570 --> 00:26:43.460
So you can say, it's like,
where is this user located?

00:26:43.460 --> 00:26:46.380
Maybe you have data
for some of your users

00:26:46.380 --> 00:26:50.100
that needs to stay within
a certain network boundary.

00:26:50.100 --> 00:26:52.950
You don't want it to leave
their organization's network.

00:26:52.950 --> 00:26:55.440
But you know that
it may potentially

00:26:55.440 --> 00:26:58.140
need to be accessed from one
of your partner organizations.

00:26:58.140 --> 00:26:59.800
And that access is allowed.

00:26:59.800 --> 00:27:02.670
So in this case, you can
set up your default network

00:27:02.670 --> 00:27:05.400
to be restricted to
one organization,

00:27:05.400 --> 00:27:07.470
block IP addresses outside that.

00:27:07.470 --> 00:27:11.700
But you can also allow accesses
from IP addresses belonging

00:27:11.700 --> 00:27:14.400
to your partner organization,
which both allows

00:27:14.400 --> 00:27:16.830
you the flexibility to
work with your partners,

00:27:16.830 --> 00:27:22.390
as well as having the security
of having data in the system.

00:27:22.390 --> 00:27:26.620
So talking about audit
and activity logging.

00:27:26.620 --> 00:27:28.570
One of the ways
Google has helped

00:27:28.570 --> 00:27:31.032
aggregate all of the
audit and activity logs

00:27:31.032 --> 00:27:32.740
is through Cloud
Security Command Center.

00:27:32.740 --> 00:27:34.740
And what Cloud Security
Command Center does

00:27:34.740 --> 00:27:38.890
is it scans through your Google
Cloud Platform resources,

00:27:38.890 --> 00:27:41.590
detects and responds to
threats in the system,

00:27:41.590 --> 00:27:44.510
and also aggregates
those for you.

00:27:44.510 --> 00:27:48.640
So when you think about issues
like misconfigured access

00:27:48.640 --> 00:27:52.540
policies, misconfigured network
policies, public storage

00:27:52.540 --> 00:27:55.960
buckets, issues that are
basically fundamental security

00:27:55.960 --> 00:27:58.210
checks that you want to be
conducting all the time,

00:27:58.210 --> 00:28:00.520
Cloud Security Command
Center is the service

00:28:00.520 --> 00:28:02.380
that would be
conducting those checks,

00:28:02.380 --> 00:28:05.950
and then surfacing that
data to your admins,

00:28:05.950 --> 00:28:07.870
to the project
owners, to make sure

00:28:07.870 --> 00:28:11.170
that the events are caught
as early as possible.

00:28:18.950 --> 00:28:21.980
One of the cool things about
Cloud Security Command Center

00:28:21.980 --> 00:28:26.010
is it was built for
a hybrid environment.

00:28:26.010 --> 00:28:28.790
So we know that people
here don't necessarily

00:28:28.790 --> 00:28:31.700
just have Google Cloud
Platform resources.

00:28:31.700 --> 00:28:34.040
People have made investments
into on-prem technologies.

00:28:34.040 --> 00:28:35.480
People are using multi-cloud.

00:28:35.480 --> 00:28:38.623
And those don't disappear the
moment you start using GCP.

00:28:38.623 --> 00:28:39.290
So we know that.

00:28:39.290 --> 00:28:42.410
We've built a number of
detectors that function well

00:28:42.410 --> 00:28:45.320
with our native
Cloud Platform tools,

00:28:45.320 --> 00:28:48.110
but we also integrate
with a number of partners

00:28:48.110 --> 00:28:50.930
who you may already be using,
who you may consider using.

00:28:50.930 --> 00:28:53.930
And we also have capabilities
to put your own monitoring

00:28:53.930 --> 00:28:55.850
data in Cloud Security
Command Center

00:28:55.850 --> 00:28:59.720
and make it that holistic audit
and logging tool for getting

00:28:59.720 --> 00:29:04.560
security and monitoring
insights into the organization.

00:29:04.560 --> 00:29:07.800
So getting towards the end of
the audit and activity logging

00:29:07.800 --> 00:29:09.240
session, I think
most people here

00:29:09.240 --> 00:29:10.650
know about Cloud Audit Logging.

00:29:10.650 --> 00:29:12.930
They know about
Access Transparency.

00:29:12.930 --> 00:29:14.760
To summarize,
Cloud Audit Logging

00:29:14.760 --> 00:29:18.270
logs your organization's
activities on your data.

00:29:18.270 --> 00:29:22.560
And Access Transparency Logging
logs the cloud provider's

00:29:22.560 --> 00:29:23.585
actions on your data.

00:29:23.585 --> 00:29:25.710
So this could be something
like an approved support

00:29:25.710 --> 00:29:28.950
activity, a bug ticket
you file for a service.

00:29:28.950 --> 00:29:31.350
If somebody at Google needs
to go in and help you resolve

00:29:31.350 --> 00:29:34.050
that ticket, you'll get an
Access Transparency log that

00:29:34.050 --> 00:29:35.370
says, this ticket was resolved.

00:29:35.370 --> 00:29:37.950
We had to access this
project for this reason.

00:29:37.950 --> 00:29:40.170
And you can basically track
everything in one place.

00:29:43.380 --> 00:29:46.200
So how we tie this all together.

00:29:46.200 --> 00:29:48.450
So we know that Google
has some responsibilities.

00:29:48.450 --> 00:29:50.950
Customers have some
responsibilities.

00:29:50.950 --> 00:29:53.580
It's important to showcase
how all of this works.

00:29:53.580 --> 00:29:54.880
And I'll do this in two parts.

00:29:54.880 --> 00:29:56.463
So I'll show you the
Cloud health care

00:29:56.463 --> 00:30:00.930
API, which is kind of a managed
service for data aggregation,

00:30:00.930 --> 00:30:04.410
data storage, data processing
on Google Cloud for health care

00:30:04.410 --> 00:30:05.040
data.

00:30:05.040 --> 00:30:07.140
And I'll also show you
how that service can

00:30:07.140 --> 00:30:11.400
fit into a larger organization
architecture with an alignment

00:30:11.400 --> 00:30:14.860
on HIPAA compliance.

00:30:14.860 --> 00:30:18.693
So overview into the
Cloud health care API.

00:30:18.693 --> 00:30:20.110
Like I said, the
Cloud health care

00:30:20.110 --> 00:30:23.540
API implements industry-standard
protocols and formats.

00:30:23.540 --> 00:30:27.370
In this case, DICOM data
for radiology records

00:30:27.370 --> 00:30:29.860
for medical imaging
data, FHIR, which

00:30:29.860 --> 00:30:32.480
is for electronic
health records text,

00:30:32.480 --> 00:30:36.120
let's say, and HL7, which
is for clinical messaging.

00:30:36.120 --> 00:30:37.900
And what we use
the cloud-- or what

00:30:37.900 --> 00:30:40.450
we hope the Cloud health
care API will help enable

00:30:40.450 --> 00:30:43.180
is accelerated ingestion,
storage, analysis,

00:30:43.180 --> 00:30:44.890
and integration of
health care data

00:30:44.890 --> 00:30:47.080
with cloud-based applications.

00:30:47.080 --> 00:30:48.920
What do I mean by that?

00:30:48.920 --> 00:30:52.780
So the health care API
provides a secure gateway

00:30:52.780 --> 00:30:58.570
from an off-cloud system into
advanced capabilities on Google

00:30:58.570 --> 00:31:01.970
Cloud, like BigQuery for
analysis, TensorFlow for ML,

00:31:01.970 --> 00:31:05.620
ML Engine for more ML, because
you can never have too much ML.

00:31:05.620 --> 00:31:10.180
And together, we hope that
this tooling and this product

00:31:10.180 --> 00:31:12.460
helps you aggregate
your data in cloud

00:31:12.460 --> 00:31:15.700
and make it available across
modalities, across formats

00:31:15.700 --> 00:31:20.050
for holistic views of patients
to enable better research

00:31:20.050 --> 00:31:21.880
and care.

00:31:21.880 --> 00:31:23.960
How does health care API look?

00:31:23.960 --> 00:31:28.480
So the health care API is just
very similar to other Cloud

00:31:28.480 --> 00:31:29.890
Storage services.

00:31:29.890 --> 00:31:33.010
It is an API that
sits in a region.

00:31:33.010 --> 00:31:38.380
So think of London or LA,
or something like that.

00:31:38.380 --> 00:31:42.220
And within that health care API
region, you have a data set.

00:31:42.220 --> 00:31:46.210
And that data set consists of
multiple stores, or buckets,

00:31:46.210 --> 00:31:48.040
of different types
of health care data.

00:31:48.040 --> 00:31:49.960
So the interesting
thing to note here

00:31:49.960 --> 00:31:53.260
is a lot of the HIPAA
compliance responsibilities have

00:31:53.260 --> 00:31:54.700
been taken care of for you.

00:31:54.700 --> 00:31:56.860
So the storage layer is done.

00:31:56.860 --> 00:31:59.170
A lot of the networking
layer is done.

00:31:59.170 --> 00:32:02.260
And the encryption layer that
comes with the storage layer

00:32:02.260 --> 00:32:03.640
is also done.

00:32:03.640 --> 00:32:05.950
And the other thing
to notice here

00:32:05.950 --> 00:32:10.660
is that data is aggregated
across different modalities

00:32:10.660 --> 00:32:12.040
in a single data set.

00:32:12.040 --> 00:32:14.050
So we have text data
that's structured.

00:32:14.050 --> 00:32:16.330
We have image data
that's structured,

00:32:16.330 --> 00:32:18.400
pixel data, and text.

00:32:18.400 --> 00:32:20.260
And we have clinical messages.

00:32:20.260 --> 00:32:22.240
And all of those in
one data set helps

00:32:22.240 --> 00:32:24.790
enable different applications.

00:32:24.790 --> 00:32:26.380
So let's imagine
that you're trying

00:32:26.380 --> 00:32:29.890
to retrieve medical imaging
data for care or for creating

00:32:29.890 --> 00:32:31.300
AI or ML models.

00:32:31.300 --> 00:32:34.435
What you might want to do here
is use the health care API

00:32:34.435 --> 00:32:38.870
as that connection piece between
an on-premise PACS or a DICOM

00:32:38.870 --> 00:32:39.370
router.

00:32:39.370 --> 00:32:42.670
A DICOM router is basically
just a fancy name for something

00:32:42.670 --> 00:32:45.550
that speaks, reads, writes
medical imaging format DICOM

00:32:45.550 --> 00:32:47.530
and connects different
systems together.

00:32:47.530 --> 00:32:49.900
And then, on the other side
of that health care API,

00:32:49.900 --> 00:32:53.290
you have your analytics and
ML modules, your application

00:32:53.290 --> 00:32:53.980
ecosystem.

00:32:53.980 --> 00:32:56.980
Potentially, this is where
you let partners access data.

00:32:56.980 --> 00:33:02.010
You let patients access data,
and anything in between.

00:33:02.010 --> 00:33:05.770
When we talk about
HL7, important

00:33:05.770 --> 00:33:10.000
in the clinical space, not
necessarily much outside that.

00:33:10.000 --> 00:33:13.240
But basically, it's the
way that different devices

00:33:13.240 --> 00:33:17.000
and operations within a hospital
communicate with each other.

00:33:17.000 --> 00:33:19.630
So if I'm a patient hooked up
to a glucose monitor or a blood

00:33:19.630 --> 00:33:21.940
pressure cuff, HL7
would be the format

00:33:21.940 --> 00:33:24.250
by which updates
for my condition

00:33:24.250 --> 00:33:27.742
get sent to my canonical
patient record.

00:33:27.742 --> 00:33:29.200
And what we've done
is we've turned

00:33:29.200 --> 00:33:32.140
this kind of weird
proprietary HL7 format

00:33:32.140 --> 00:33:33.790
into a structured JSON.

00:33:33.790 --> 00:33:36.760
What that does is it helps make
it available for analytics,

00:33:36.760 --> 00:33:39.910
research, you name it.

00:33:39.910 --> 00:33:41.920
So where's this all going?

00:33:41.920 --> 00:33:47.890
Why is this important to the
HIPAA security presentation?

00:33:47.890 --> 00:33:49.540
What we're doing
with the health care

00:33:49.540 --> 00:33:53.140
API is translating data
in multiple formats

00:33:53.140 --> 00:33:55.780
to a canonical standard
format, and then

00:33:55.780 --> 00:33:59.410
making that data available to
the rest of Google Cloud, all

00:33:59.410 --> 00:34:01.450
of the rest of the Google
Cloud HIPAA-compliant

00:34:01.450 --> 00:34:03.580
or HIPAA-aligned services.

00:34:03.580 --> 00:34:06.310
And what this
enables is it enables

00:34:06.310 --> 00:34:09.670
you to reduce the burden of
setting this up yourself.

00:34:09.670 --> 00:34:12.070
It enables you to reduce
the burden of setting up

00:34:12.070 --> 00:34:15.580
the security and compliance
for this translation layer,

00:34:15.580 --> 00:34:18.130
for the storage layer
yourself, and then

00:34:18.130 --> 00:34:22.480
lets you concentrate on doing
what I would call core business

00:34:22.480 --> 00:34:23.500
activities.

00:34:23.500 --> 00:34:26.110
I think in a lot
of cases, security

00:34:26.110 --> 00:34:28.179
is table stakes for
an organization.

00:34:28.179 --> 00:34:30.190
Security is something
you have to get right.

00:34:30.190 --> 00:34:32.770
But security is
not the end goal.

00:34:32.770 --> 00:34:34.630
Why are we securing data?

00:34:34.630 --> 00:34:37.239
It is so we can do
something with it.

00:34:37.239 --> 00:34:39.130
And that something
can be research.

00:34:39.130 --> 00:34:40.630
That something can
be patient care.

00:34:40.630 --> 00:34:44.980
That something can be improving
the quality of treatments.

00:34:44.980 --> 00:34:47.770
But securing the data
for the sake of security

00:34:47.770 --> 00:34:49.360
is generally not the end goal.

00:34:49.360 --> 00:34:52.060
And what we're doing here is
wrapping that security layer

00:34:52.060 --> 00:34:57.280
into, I guess, a product layer
that can do other things.

00:35:00.610 --> 00:35:04.960
I think talking about
what some people consider

00:35:04.960 --> 00:35:07.060
the holy grail of
research is being

00:35:07.060 --> 00:35:10.870
able to see longitudinal patient
data from multiple sources

00:35:10.870 --> 00:35:13.940
in multiple formats
in one place.

00:35:13.940 --> 00:35:15.940
I'm not saying we've solved it.

00:35:15.940 --> 00:35:18.460
I'm just saying
there's a possibility

00:35:18.460 --> 00:35:19.930
that it can be done.

00:35:19.930 --> 00:35:22.090
So you have data from
multiple formats.

00:35:22.090 --> 00:35:23.950
You want to get it into
the health care API.

00:35:23.950 --> 00:35:25.742
And then you want to
turn it into BigQuery,

00:35:25.742 --> 00:35:28.990
which is where you'll get
your SQL-friendly Query

00:35:28.990 --> 00:35:31.990
language across all
of these domains which

00:35:31.990 --> 00:35:33.685
used to have different formats.

00:35:37.040 --> 00:35:38.610
From a security
purpose, maybe you

00:35:38.610 --> 00:35:40.610
want to deidentify your
data before you share it

00:35:40.610 --> 00:35:41.510
with other people.

00:35:41.510 --> 00:35:45.410
The health care API supports
native deidentification

00:35:45.410 --> 00:35:47.360
of data in these FHIR formats.

00:35:47.360 --> 00:35:50.390
So what we've done is we've
taken the structure of FHIR,

00:35:50.390 --> 00:35:52.400
parsed it, and said,
we know this field

00:35:52.400 --> 00:35:54.240
contains patient names.

00:35:54.240 --> 00:35:56.720
So if you want to take out
patient names, we can do that.

00:35:56.720 --> 00:35:58.915
We know some fields
contain dates.

00:35:58.915 --> 00:36:00.290
If those dates
need to be removed

00:36:00.290 --> 00:36:02.120
to protect patient
privacy or shifted

00:36:02.120 --> 00:36:04.400
to maintain patient
privacy, we can do that.

00:36:04.400 --> 00:36:06.800
What's not shown
here is free text.

00:36:06.800 --> 00:36:08.250
So free text is another--

00:36:08.250 --> 00:36:11.810
they're long strings
of provider notes.

00:36:11.810 --> 00:36:15.920
We can do that as well, it
just isn't on this slide.

00:36:15.920 --> 00:36:17.660
Talking about doing
that in DICOM.

00:36:17.660 --> 00:36:20.570
DICOM files contain
patient data in metadata--

00:36:20.570 --> 00:36:22.320
or sorry, patient
information in metadata,

00:36:22.320 --> 00:36:23.987
which you see in the
top left and right,

00:36:23.987 --> 00:36:26.960
and patient data burnt
into the pixel data

00:36:26.960 --> 00:36:28.890
in the middle-bottom.

00:36:28.890 --> 00:36:31.640
Our deidentification tools
can also strip that out.

00:36:31.640 --> 00:36:33.540
And thinking about
HIPAA compliance,

00:36:33.540 --> 00:36:38.870
this is a fundamental ability
in data processing and data

00:36:38.870 --> 00:36:39.547
protection.

00:36:39.547 --> 00:36:41.630
So when you're sending
data between organizations,

00:36:41.630 --> 00:36:43.640
one of the things that's
important to HIPAA

00:36:43.640 --> 00:36:45.800
is data is protected in transit.

00:36:45.800 --> 00:36:48.350
And one of the ways you can
protect patient data in transit

00:36:48.350 --> 00:36:51.840
is to not send any patient
data in the first place.

00:36:51.840 --> 00:36:54.590
So deidentifying the
files before they're

00:36:54.590 --> 00:36:57.250
sent between organizations,
before they're published,

00:36:57.250 --> 00:37:00.980
is a great way to meet
the HIPAA requirements

00:37:00.980 --> 00:37:03.083
while still meeting the
business requirements.

00:37:05.630 --> 00:37:07.700
Finally, tying it all together.

00:37:07.700 --> 00:37:10.820
How can something
like this look?

00:37:10.820 --> 00:37:13.430
So the sample that
I have here is--

00:37:13.430 --> 00:37:16.070
the user journey is
you have some data that

00:37:16.070 --> 00:37:19.250
exists off of Google Cloud.

00:37:19.250 --> 00:37:22.880
Perhaps, this is on-prem, in a
colo in your own data center,

00:37:22.880 --> 00:37:25.340
on a third-party infrastructure,
on another cloud,

00:37:25.340 --> 00:37:27.590
on a managed service provider.

00:37:27.590 --> 00:37:30.410
There's quite a number
of options here.

00:37:30.410 --> 00:37:32.870
And you want to move
that data to Cloud,

00:37:32.870 --> 00:37:36.660
get a canonical data
storage platform,

00:37:36.660 --> 00:37:40.520
then make that data available
to research partners,

00:37:40.520 --> 00:37:44.150
to your own organization,
to third-party services,

00:37:44.150 --> 00:37:45.590
and anything in between.

00:37:45.590 --> 00:37:48.710
But at the same
time, maintain HIPAA.

00:37:48.710 --> 00:37:51.290
That's kind of the
key methodology here,

00:37:51.290 --> 00:37:53.130
or maintain GDPR.

00:37:53.130 --> 00:37:57.240
The controls can change, but
the intent stays the same.

00:37:57.240 --> 00:38:00.860
So the way I've broken
this up here is into four,

00:38:00.860 --> 00:38:02.318
what we call, GCP projects.

00:38:02.318 --> 00:38:04.610
And that's the data ingestion
project, the data storage

00:38:04.610 --> 00:38:06.800
and analysis project,
the data sharing project,

00:38:06.800 --> 00:38:08.930
and the monitoring
and auditing project.

00:38:08.930 --> 00:38:12.350
I've included some underlying
management services here,

00:38:12.350 --> 00:38:15.470
like our logging platform,
our IAM platform, what

00:38:15.470 --> 00:38:19.700
we call our StackDriver services
for debugging and monitoring.

00:38:19.700 --> 00:38:21.590
Not because they're
in a separate project,

00:38:21.590 --> 00:38:24.500
but just because they underlie
all of the other ones and I

00:38:24.500 --> 00:38:28.330
didn't want to duplicate
them in all of the above.

00:38:28.330 --> 00:38:31.520
So again, so now we've
talked about the use case.

00:38:31.520 --> 00:38:33.590
Why is this use case relevant?

00:38:33.590 --> 00:38:37.280
This is what we think as
one of the fundamental user

00:38:37.280 --> 00:38:40.170
journeys for cloud migration.

00:38:40.170 --> 00:38:43.340
You'll want to take data from
multiple disparate sources,

00:38:43.340 --> 00:38:45.770
ingest it, normalize it,
aggregate it, and then make

00:38:45.770 --> 00:38:47.420
it available for use.

00:38:50.900 --> 00:38:53.690
So breaking this down
into individual steps.

00:38:53.690 --> 00:38:57.030
How does HIPAA kind of interplay
in all of these things?

00:38:57.030 --> 00:38:59.520
So this is our first
project, data ingestion.

00:38:59.520 --> 00:39:02.060
So the data ingestion
layer is where

00:39:02.060 --> 00:39:05.210
you'll move raw patient
data onto Google Cloud,

00:39:05.210 --> 00:39:10.370
perform an ETL operation,
extract, transform, and load,

00:39:10.370 --> 00:39:12.590
and basically change
that data from one

00:39:12.590 --> 00:39:14.390
format in its native
on-premise format

00:39:14.390 --> 00:39:16.070
to a cloud-native format.

00:39:16.070 --> 00:39:18.950
And here, the protections
that would be in place

00:39:18.950 --> 00:39:20.390
is it's temporary storage.

00:39:20.390 --> 00:39:24.230
It's ephemeral machines that
are doing transformation.

00:39:24.230 --> 00:39:27.035
And it's on a separate network
because we don't want--

00:39:29.670 --> 00:39:32.360
the network that goes
from on-premise to Cloud,

00:39:32.360 --> 00:39:35.840
we don't want to contaminate
it by giving it access too far.

00:39:40.490 --> 00:39:43.010
Now that we've ingested
and normalized the data,

00:39:43.010 --> 00:39:47.120
we probably want to move it
to a canonical data store.

00:39:47.120 --> 00:39:50.330
And here is what some people
would call a data lake, a data

00:39:50.330 --> 00:39:51.440
aggregation layer.

00:39:51.440 --> 00:39:52.460
It does storage.

00:39:52.460 --> 00:39:53.580
It does analysis.

00:39:53.580 --> 00:39:56.660
It might be where you serve
some of the ML applications.

00:39:56.660 --> 00:39:59.060
It writes logs to
the audit project.

00:39:59.060 --> 00:40:02.990
And most importantly, no
external network connectivity.

00:40:02.990 --> 00:40:06.980
So this means that your
network for this application

00:40:06.980 --> 00:40:10.200
is locked down to only
your organization.

00:40:10.200 --> 00:40:12.770
It means that the
core data layer

00:40:12.770 --> 00:40:17.480
is only accessible by services
and service accounts that

00:40:17.480 --> 00:40:20.030
are providing-- those service
accounts may be providing

00:40:20.030 --> 00:40:26.480
public services, but no public
access to this project directly

00:40:26.480 --> 00:40:29.730
for obvious reasons.

00:40:29.730 --> 00:40:31.490
But there does come
a time when you

00:40:31.490 --> 00:40:34.250
do want to provide
services externally.

00:40:34.250 --> 00:40:38.150
This, in some cases, can be
done in a different project.

00:40:38.150 --> 00:40:41.210
This may be done
in a different VPC.

00:40:41.210 --> 00:40:43.608
Here, we've shown it as both.

00:40:43.608 --> 00:40:45.650
It's in a different project
with its own network.

00:40:45.650 --> 00:40:48.500
This project can connect
through service accounts

00:40:48.500 --> 00:40:52.370
to the data aggregation
layer, and it can also

00:40:52.370 --> 00:40:55.550
connect to external services.

00:40:55.550 --> 00:40:59.210
So maybe you want to serve
an ML model publicly.

00:40:59.210 --> 00:41:02.240
So this service would
expose a public end point.

00:41:02.240 --> 00:41:05.030
Somebody can make an
API request to it.

00:41:05.030 --> 00:41:09.390
It would then send the service
account back to this project.

00:41:09.390 --> 00:41:11.780
That service account request
would go grab something

00:41:11.780 --> 00:41:13.490
from the underlying data set.

00:41:13.490 --> 00:41:15.260
It would bring it
back to this project,

00:41:15.260 --> 00:41:18.590
aggregate it, and
share it publicly.

00:41:18.590 --> 00:41:24.480
So what we've left out so far
is the big component of HIPAA

00:41:24.480 --> 00:41:27.140
is centralized logging
and monitoring.

00:41:27.140 --> 00:41:31.040
If any patient data is touched,
there should be a log entry.

00:41:31.040 --> 00:41:34.070
If that log entry
is flagged, there

00:41:34.070 --> 00:41:37.513
needs to be manual
auditing of the activity

00:41:37.513 --> 00:41:39.680
to make sure that it was
legitimate or illegitimate,

00:41:39.680 --> 00:41:42.470
and then appropriate
action is taken afterwards.

00:41:42.470 --> 00:41:46.520
So what this project is doing is
simplifying all that monitoring

00:41:46.520 --> 00:41:47.750
for you.

00:41:47.750 --> 00:41:50.150
I won't say for you, but
simplifying all that monitoring

00:41:50.150 --> 00:41:51.270
in one place.

00:41:51.270 --> 00:41:53.960
So all of the other projects
are sending audit logs here.

00:41:53.960 --> 00:41:56.270
All of the other projects
are sending system logs here,

00:41:56.270 --> 00:41:57.122
monitoring data.

00:41:57.122 --> 00:41:58.580
And then in this
project, you would

00:41:58.580 --> 00:42:02.030
put your rules, your
triggers, your alerts,

00:42:02.030 --> 00:42:05.810
your notifications, such that
not only is it seeing activity

00:42:05.810 --> 00:42:07.340
from individual
projects, it's also

00:42:07.340 --> 00:42:08.810
seeing activity holistically.

00:42:08.810 --> 00:42:12.170
So it can account for trends
across different things

00:42:12.170 --> 00:42:13.250
in the environment.

00:42:13.250 --> 00:42:16.400
And the benefit of aggregating
all these logs together

00:42:16.400 --> 00:42:18.890
is specifically that.

00:42:18.890 --> 00:42:21.380
Now you get access
to full trends,

00:42:21.380 --> 00:42:24.080
to patterns, to
longitudinal accesses,

00:42:24.080 --> 00:42:26.510
and how data moves
throughout the system.

00:42:26.510 --> 00:42:29.180
Whereas if you were just looking
at logs from a single project,

00:42:29.180 --> 00:42:30.097
you wouldn't get that.

00:42:34.890 --> 00:42:39.750
This is probably some, I
guess, of my opinions on best

00:42:39.750 --> 00:42:41.140
practices for audit logging.

00:42:41.140 --> 00:42:43.800
It's what we recommend
for internal teams.

00:42:43.800 --> 00:42:46.710
It's what we recommend to some
of our customers and partners

00:42:46.710 --> 00:42:48.480
as well.

00:42:48.480 --> 00:42:50.130
Turn on data access logging.

00:42:50.130 --> 00:42:53.010
So Cloud, we know, has two
forms of audit logging, admin

00:42:53.010 --> 00:42:54.900
activity, which
is on by default,

00:42:54.900 --> 00:42:57.780
and data access logging,
which captures access to data.

00:42:57.780 --> 00:43:00.150
So turn on data access
logging for services

00:43:00.150 --> 00:43:02.810
that are holding PHI.

00:43:02.810 --> 00:43:04.050
Set up audit log export.

00:43:04.050 --> 00:43:06.120
We talked about that,
and why it's important.

00:43:06.120 --> 00:43:10.500
And configure access control
for logs appropriately.

00:43:10.500 --> 00:43:12.690
You want to avoid
the situation where

00:43:12.690 --> 00:43:14.790
somebody who did malicious
things to your data

00:43:14.790 --> 00:43:18.580
can then go delete the
audit logs for that.

00:43:18.580 --> 00:43:22.310
And lastly, you actually want
to look at the audit logs.

00:43:22.310 --> 00:43:22.890
Also obvious.

00:43:28.434 --> 00:43:32.610
This process of making
a HIPAA-aligned project

00:43:32.610 --> 00:43:37.210
architecture is something
that is not impossible,

00:43:37.210 --> 00:43:39.030
but it is common.

00:43:39.030 --> 00:43:42.300
And when our team looked
at all of these activities

00:43:42.300 --> 00:43:44.100
and said, what are
the things that

00:43:44.100 --> 00:43:47.190
go into making a
HIPAA-aligned project,

00:43:47.190 --> 00:43:49.740
it was basically things like
controlling access, controlling

00:43:49.740 --> 00:43:52.282
encryption, controlling network
boundaries, controlling audit

00:43:52.282 --> 00:43:53.260
logs.

00:43:53.260 --> 00:43:58.230
So what we did was we created
a set of open-source tooling.

00:43:58.230 --> 00:44:00.990
We're unofficially calling it
the Data Protection Toolkit.

00:44:00.990 --> 00:44:03.060
And it allows for
infrastructure-as-code

00:44:03.060 --> 00:44:05.730
deployment of projects
that are designed

00:44:05.730 --> 00:44:08.670
to meet some sort of
organizational regulatory

00:44:08.670 --> 00:44:09.990
compliance requirements.

00:44:09.990 --> 00:44:13.140
So it leverages
Deployment Manager,

00:44:13.140 --> 00:44:16.530
which is a HIPAA-aligned
service and soon

00:44:16.530 --> 00:44:19.140
to be Terraform, which is a
popular open-source toolkit as

00:44:19.140 --> 00:44:20.610
well.

00:44:20.610 --> 00:44:25.410
What this actually
does is it aggregates

00:44:25.410 --> 00:44:29.400
an entire scope of HIPAA
activities into one toolkit.

00:44:29.400 --> 00:44:33.012
So what you'll do is you'll
define what resources you want.

00:44:33.012 --> 00:44:35.220
You want some compute, some
storage, some networking.

00:44:35.220 --> 00:44:37.950
And you'll define the controls
that you want in place.

00:44:37.950 --> 00:44:40.200
Some resources
shouldn't be public.

00:44:40.200 --> 00:44:44.160
Some resources should
prevent data access.

00:44:44.160 --> 00:44:47.040
Some resources should generate
more logs than others.

00:44:47.040 --> 00:44:50.340
And some resources should
be allowed to be shared.

00:44:50.340 --> 00:44:52.470
You're basically--
there's a controls library

00:44:52.470 --> 00:44:53.880
and a resources library.

00:44:53.880 --> 00:44:55.470
And you will choose
the specific scope

00:44:55.470 --> 00:44:56.860
of resources you're
interested in

00:44:56.860 --> 00:44:59.520
and the specific scope of
policies you're interested in.

00:44:59.520 --> 00:45:02.790
And this service will
automatically deploy them

00:45:02.790 --> 00:45:04.890
and create a GCP project.

00:45:04.890 --> 00:45:07.710
At the same time as
doing so, it will also

00:45:07.710 --> 00:45:09.900
put a continuous
monitoring framework

00:45:09.900 --> 00:45:11.550
around those resources.

00:45:11.550 --> 00:45:14.100
And what that continuous
monitoring framework will do

00:45:14.100 --> 00:45:16.530
is periodically
interrogate the resources

00:45:16.530 --> 00:45:19.350
you set up against the
policies that you set up

00:45:19.350 --> 00:45:21.990
and give you data.

00:45:21.990 --> 00:45:24.450
That data can be, yes,
everything's good,

00:45:24.450 --> 00:45:26.430
or that data can be,
here's a violation.

00:45:26.430 --> 00:45:28.590
You should probably
look into it.

00:45:28.590 --> 00:45:31.830
And what that enables is to
meet the HIPAA requirements

00:45:31.830 --> 00:45:38.340
of audit and monitoring all
from one continuous tool chain.

00:45:38.340 --> 00:45:41.730
Why did we choose Forseti
for the monitoring engine?

00:45:41.730 --> 00:45:44.820
So for those of you who aren't
familiar with Forseti security,

00:45:44.820 --> 00:45:47.520
it's a native
open-source tooling

00:45:47.520 --> 00:45:50.970
for Google Cloud that builds
an inventory of projects,

00:45:50.970 --> 00:45:53.670
scans it repeatedly
and periodically

00:45:53.670 --> 00:45:55.530
for a set of policies.

00:45:55.530 --> 00:45:58.680
And in a certain
case of policies,

00:45:58.680 --> 00:46:02.260
actually enforces against
malicious changes.

00:46:02.260 --> 00:46:05.490
So if you set an inventory--

00:46:05.490 --> 00:46:08.190
when we think back
to here, if you

00:46:08.190 --> 00:46:10.020
said, in the
deployment stage, you

00:46:10.020 --> 00:46:13.020
want three VMs and
two storage buckets,

00:46:13.020 --> 00:46:15.450
this inventory is
going to get created.

00:46:15.450 --> 00:46:18.360
And hopefully, it should contain
three VMs and two storage

00:46:18.360 --> 00:46:19.110
buckets.

00:46:19.110 --> 00:46:21.900
And if it doesn't contain three
VMs and two storage buckets,

00:46:21.900 --> 00:46:25.230
that scanner is going to pick it
up and send you an alert so you

00:46:25.230 --> 00:46:27.180
can go back and correct it.

00:46:27.180 --> 00:46:30.240
And all of this, over
time, is kind of saying,

00:46:30.240 --> 00:46:33.390
if you defined your HIPAA
policies a certain way,

00:46:33.390 --> 00:46:36.960
as your organization grows,
you may need more resources,

00:46:36.960 --> 00:46:38.730
but new resources
that you create

00:46:38.730 --> 00:46:41.970
will be under the
same policy framework

00:46:41.970 --> 00:46:46.070
that you set up originally.

00:46:46.070 --> 00:46:49.110
So why we think this tooling
is helpful to customers.

00:46:49.110 --> 00:46:51.510
It's a secure, what
we call, quick start.

00:46:51.510 --> 00:46:55.530
As I was saying, security
is fundamentally important,

00:46:55.530 --> 00:46:57.690
but it's not necessarily
the end goal.

00:46:57.690 --> 00:47:00.600
So this is a framework
to help people get up

00:47:00.600 --> 00:47:03.150
and running quickly
on GCP by creating

00:47:03.150 --> 00:47:06.270
predictable, consistent, secure
workloads that then let you

00:47:06.270 --> 00:47:08.290
do your business requirements.

00:47:08.290 --> 00:47:10.050
So this is saying,
if you have to create

00:47:10.050 --> 00:47:12.900
identical development, testing,
and production environments

00:47:12.900 --> 00:47:14.490
to run a medical
device because that's

00:47:14.490 --> 00:47:16.620
the regulatory
framework, this tool will

00:47:16.620 --> 00:47:19.302
allow you to write one
template, run it three times.

00:47:19.302 --> 00:47:20.760
The other thing it
allows you to do

00:47:20.760 --> 00:47:22.740
is if you have a
canonical data layer

00:47:22.740 --> 00:47:26.025
and you want to give
researchers or partners access

00:47:26.025 --> 00:47:28.400
to that data, and you want to
give it to them in a locked

00:47:28.400 --> 00:47:30.210
down environment,
these templates

00:47:30.210 --> 00:47:33.360
will let you spin up, lock
down identical environments

00:47:33.360 --> 00:47:36.432
for each research group
that's accessing the data.

00:47:36.432 --> 00:47:37.890
And the other thing
is because this

00:47:37.890 --> 00:47:40.260
is an infrastructure-as-code
framework,

00:47:40.260 --> 00:47:42.630
it's easy to share these
templates across teams

00:47:42.630 --> 00:47:45.270
and across institutions.

00:47:45.270 --> 00:47:48.390
So now talking about some
customers who are successfully

00:47:48.390 --> 00:47:52.320
running HIPAA-aligned workloads
on Google Cloud, three examples

00:47:52.320 --> 00:47:54.090
generally come to
mind that showcase

00:47:54.090 --> 00:47:56.550
the range of activities.

00:47:56.550 --> 00:48:01.230
So the University of
Colorado, Health Data Compass,

00:48:01.230 --> 00:48:05.580
has used Google Cloud to achieve
HIPAA compliance on a data

00:48:05.580 --> 00:48:06.480
warehouse.

00:48:06.480 --> 00:48:11.670
And this has helped them reduce
query times from many hours

00:48:11.670 --> 00:48:16.020
to just a few minutes, and has
helped them cut operating costs

00:48:16.020 --> 00:48:18.510
and make their research
programs more scalable.

00:48:18.510 --> 00:48:21.180
And this is, again,
following the model

00:48:21.180 --> 00:48:22.500
of shared responsibility.

00:48:22.500 --> 00:48:24.750
So previously, they were
managing all of this on-prem.

00:48:24.750 --> 00:48:28.470
By moving their infrastructure
to Google Cloud,

00:48:28.470 --> 00:48:30.660
they cut their
compliance responsibility

00:48:30.660 --> 00:48:33.990
by a significant portion
and realized other benefits

00:48:33.990 --> 00:48:36.780
at the same time, leading
to faster research.

00:48:39.300 --> 00:48:42.480
So a second example.

00:48:42.480 --> 00:48:44.190
You'll start to see
a trend develop.

00:48:44.190 --> 00:48:47.670
Move workloads to Cloud
helps reduce responsibility,

00:48:47.670 --> 00:48:50.670
helps reallocate more
bandwidth, money, energy,

00:48:50.670 --> 00:48:54.720
time to other activities,
leading to the acceleration

00:48:54.720 --> 00:48:56.620
of the core business
requirements.

00:48:56.620 --> 00:49:00.270
So specifically for the NIH
National Institute on Aging,

00:49:00.270 --> 00:49:04.890
they were able to process
200 terabytes of data

00:49:04.890 --> 00:49:06.900
in just a few weeks,
which would normally

00:49:06.900 --> 00:49:08.190
have taken them months.

00:49:08.190 --> 00:49:10.800
This is an institution
that had hardware

00:49:10.800 --> 00:49:12.330
that was potentially older.

00:49:12.330 --> 00:49:16.200
They used the same amount of
funds on Cloud-based hardware

00:49:16.200 --> 00:49:19.470
and process that
data much faster.

00:49:19.470 --> 00:49:24.450
And lastly, the Broad Institute
is doing genomic analysis

00:49:24.450 --> 00:49:25.830
on Google Cloud.

00:49:25.830 --> 00:49:29.100
And moving their
infrastructure here

00:49:29.100 --> 00:49:32.880
has helped them accelerate
the analysis of human genomes

00:49:32.880 --> 00:49:34.240
by 400%.

00:49:34.240 --> 00:49:37.110
They've instituted new and
different security protections

00:49:37.110 --> 00:49:39.510
than they were
able to do on-prem.

00:49:39.510 --> 00:49:42.330
And again, the trend continues.

00:49:42.330 --> 00:49:45.930
Allocated resources better
led to more efficient research

00:49:45.930 --> 00:49:47.880
patterns.

00:49:47.880 --> 00:49:50.760
And with that, thank you
for joining us today.

00:49:50.760 --> 00:49:52.800
And I'll be around to
take any questions.

00:49:52.800 --> 00:49:53.700
